               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>What is a Quipu?</h3>A <i>quipu</i> (also written <i>khipu</i>) is a system of knotted cords used in the Andes to record and transmit information. Its origin is probably ancient with investigations showing it was used in Caral, in present-day Peru (<htcite0> ; <htcite1> ; <htcite2>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "720f6dfd-4b86-4e07-a887-1010e1041a4d",
                     "text": "Ruta Caral",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "03c3743c-d268-4730-ba8e-d36b5c49e781",
                     "text": "Youtube - Caral Perú Oficial",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c2ff41fc-7833-4dc2-8882-eaed12a065b7",
                     "text": "Mann, Charles C.",
                     "page": "pp. 1008",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2005",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgQuipuPanel\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgQuipuPanel', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Panel at the Caral archaeological site, © ZonaArqueólogicaCaral (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>). Photo taken on <htdate2>, during a visit to the archaeological site of Caral.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "720f6dfd-4b86-4e07-a887-1010e1041a4d",
                     "text": "Ruta Caral",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "03c3743c-d268-4730-ba8e-d36b5c49e781",
                     "text": "Youtube - Caral Perú Oficial",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2017",
                     "month": "6",
                     "day": "13"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Panel at the Caral archaeological site showing a person holding a quipu and a Yupana board.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>A quipu has a main cord from which several pendant cords hang. On each cord, numbers are recorded with knots placed in <b>orders</b> from the bottom upward: the first order holds the units (0 to 9), the next order holds the tens (10 to 99), then the hundreds (100 to 999), and so on. The absence of a knot in an order means zero, each knot adds one unit to that order, and a single order never holds more than nine knots (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4f8542c9-f060-4d3c-8a67-971b6d63e1ff",
                     "text": "Ascher, M., & Ascher, R.",
                     "page": "pp. 29 - 32",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6e9bef8c-6ec5-4c04-b583-aea8e6bbec5b",
                     "text": "Quesada, Eugenio de",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2016",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "04"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>The <b>order</b> of each knot is what gives it its value. One knot in the first order is worth one, but the same knot in the next order is worth ten, and in the order above that it is worth one hundred. For this reason, order is what allows a quipu to represent a precise number: four knots in the tens order and seven in the units order mean 47, while seven tens and four units mean 74. Keeping the correct order is what makes the number exact (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4f8542c9-f060-4d3c-8a67-971b6d63e1ff",
                     "text": "Ascher, M., & Ascher, R.",
                     "page": "pp. 29 - 32",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_quipu_text",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h4>Quipus and writing</h4>For a long time it was thought that the Andes had no writing. Today, several researchers propose that the <i>quipus</i> may have recorded much more than numbers: they may also have kept words and narratives, that is, a form of writing (<htcite0> ; <htcite1> ; <htcite2> ; <htcite3> ; <htcite4>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c2ff41fc-7833-4dc2-8882-eaed12a065b7",
                     "text": "Mann, Charles C.",
                     "page": "pp. 1008",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2005",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2ebd3420-941b-4f17-b5bc-17bb39ef37a0",
                     "text": "Hyland, S.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2017",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b74bf294-c758-4a48-88c9-399ca4f021f6",
                     "text": "Cartwright, M.",
                     "page": "Quipu",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2014",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "08"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6ff52558-f4fb-49cd-a737-0e2f3863ae7b",
                     "text": "Laurencich Minelli, L.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2016",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "75ca7e93-6ffd-4303-b21d-b669592dcde3",
                     "text": "Urton, G.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2019",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_game",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Represent numbers with the Quipu</h3>It is your turn. Add knots with the arrows to build each number. Build the number from the units upward: when an order is complete, the game jumps immediately to the next order on the string. When a level is complete, click 'Next level' to move on or 'New number' to redo that level. Remember that the absence of a knot means zero.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p style=\"display: flex; gap: 8px; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap;\"><span id=\"quipuLevelBadge\" class=\"level-badge\">Level 1</span> <button type=\"button\" id=\"quipuNewBtn\" class=\"reset-tutor-btn\">🎲 New number</button> <button type=\"button\" id=\"quipuNextLevelBtn\" class=\"reset-tutor-btn\" style=\"background: #7e5a41;\">⭐ Next level</button></p><div id=\"quipuBoard\" class=\"quipuBoard\"></div><p id=\"quipuMessage\" class=\"quipuMessage\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p><p id=\"quipuCongrats\" class=\"quipuCongrats\" style=\"display: none;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-medal\" style=\"color: gold;\"></i> <span id=\"quipuCongratsText\"></span></p><span id=\"quipuLevelLabel\" style=\"display: none;\">Level</span><span id=\"quipuOrder0\" style=\"display: none;\">Units</span><span id=\"quipuOrder1\" style=\"display: none;\">Tens</span><span id=\"quipuOrder2\" style=\"display: none;\">Hundreds</span><span id=\"quipuMsgOrder\" style=\"display: none;\">Order complete! The game jumps to the next order.</span><span id=\"quipuMsgString\" style=\"display: none;\">Number complete! Now continue with the next string.</span><span id=\"quipuMsgLevel\" style=\"display: none;\">🎉 Level complete! Click 'Next level' to continue or 'New number' to redo this level.</span><span id=\"quipuMsgAllLevels\" style=\"display: none;\">🎉🏆 ALL LEVELS COMPLETE! 🏆🎉 You built every number! Click 'Next level' to start again at level 1 or 'New number' to redo this level.</span>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\">The rules for representing numbers with knots follow the description of Marcia Ascher and Robert Ascher (<htcite0>), and the vertical order studied in <i>Change of direction (Part II)</i> (<htcite1>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4f8542c9-f060-4d3c-8a67-971b6d63e1ff",
                     "text": "Ascher, M., & Ascher, R.",
